## Problem

A site-wide mobile hamburger menu rendered as a ~84px strip instead of a full-screen overlay, with several menu links landing off-screen and physically untappable (`document.elementFromPoint` returns `null` at their coordinates). Desktop was unaffected. The class-toggle JS (`nav-open` add/remove, `aria-expanded`) worked perfectly — screenshots showed the overlay background only covering a thin strip at the top, with remaining links overlapping un-dimmed page content. Reproduced identically in Chromium and WebKit, so not a browser bug.

## Root cause

The nav bar had a frosted-glass effect: `nav { position: fixed; backdrop-filter: blur(12px); }`. Per CSS spec (filter effects / css-transforms), **`backdrop-filter` — like `filter`, `transform`, `perspective`, and `will-change` naming those — establishes a new containing block for `position: fixed` descendants.** The mobile menu (`.nav-links { position: fixed; top:0; left:0; right:0; bottom:0 }`) was a DOM child of the nav, so its `inset: 0` resolved against the nav's own ~84px box instead of the viewport. The flex-centered links were then centered inside that 84px box, spilling above and below the real viewport.

This is insidious because: (a) the glass effect and the menu are usually added at different times by different changes, and the breakage appears only when both exist; (b) toggle logic, ARIA state, and desktop layout all stay green; (c) it is invisible to any test that only asserts the class was applied.

## Fix (zero HTML/JS changes)

Move the visual effect to a pseudo-element, which cannot contain descendants:

```css
nav#main-nav { /* remove background + backdrop-filter from here */ }
nav#main-nav::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: -1;
  pointer-events: none;
  background: ;
  backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
}
```

Stacking is safe when the nav itself carries an explicit `z-index` (e.g. `position: fixed; z-index: 100`): that unconditionally establishes a stacking context (CSS 2.1 rule, no reliance on newer "fixed creates a context" behavior), so the `z-index: -1` pseudo is trapped inside the nav's context — it paints above page content but below the nav's own children, and can never slip under the page background. `pointer-events: none` prevents click interception. Alternatives if you prefer restructuring: make the menu a sibling of the nav, or portal it to `document.body` on open.

## Verification pattern

- Measured `.nav-links` `getBoundingClientRect().height` with the menu open at 375x812: viewport-exact after the fix (was 84px).
- Hit-tested every link center via `elementFromPoint`.
- Desktop before/after screenshot pixel-diff of the nav region: 0 differing pixels (the glass look is preserved exactly).
- Regression test idea that actually catches recurrence: statically parse the stylesheet and assert **no ancestor of the fixed overlay's selector carries `backdrop-filter` / `filter` / `transform` / `will-change` / `perspective`** (strip comments first so an explanatory comment naming the property doesn't false-trigger). A class-toggle assertion will NOT catch this bug class.

## Caveats

- The pseudo-element route assumes the nav has (or can be given) an explicit z-index; without one, verify stacking in a scrolled state.
- A static ancestor-scan test won't catch a re-trap introduced via inline `style=` or JS-injected styles — full protection needs a headless-browser geometry assertion (bounding-rect height ≈ viewport height when open).
- Same trap applies to any `position: fixed` child: dropdowns, toasts, modals mounted inside a filtered/transformed ancestor.
